    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p44-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{3x-5}{2x}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\cos\\theta=\\frac{3x-5}{2x}",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use the perimeter to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=6-x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then apply the cosine rule.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=10-4-x\\\\y&=6-x\\end{align*}$$\nBy the cosine rule,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{x^2+4^2-(6-x)^2}{2(x)(4)}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{12x-20}{8x}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{3x-5}{2x}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Let the area of the triangle be $A\\text{ cm}^2$. Show that\n$$A^2=-5x^2+30x-25.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "A^2=-5x^2+30x-25",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Use Pythagoras to obtain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", then square the triangle area formula.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Pythagoras on the perpendicular triangle gives $\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin^2\\theta&=1-\\left(\\frac{3x-5}{2x}\\right)^2\\\\\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac{4x^2-(3x-5)^2}{4x^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing $A=\\frac12(4)x\\sin\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}A^2&=\\frac14(4)^2x^2\\sin^2\\theta\\\\A^2&=4x^2\\left(\\frac{4x^2-(3x-5)^2}{4x^2}\\right)\\\\A^2&=4x^2-(3x-5)^2\\\\A^2&=-5x^2+30x-25.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      },
      {
        "id": "p44-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the largest possible area of the triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sqrt{20}\\text{ cm}^2",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p44-q9-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The quadratic has maximum ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "A^2=20",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=3",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}A^2&=-5x^2+30x-25\\\\A^2&=-5(x-3)^2+20.\\end{align*}$$\nThis concave-down quadratic has its maximum at $x=3$, giving $A^2=20$. The side lengths are then $3$, $3$ and $4$ cm, which form a triangle.\n$$A_{\\max}=\\sqrt{20}\\text{ cm}^2.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
